"narrative":"No assessments are due. Sign convention for these proposals: for direction -1 SMARCA4-loss and PTEN-loss mean-difference claims, a positive signed estimate means the loss group has a more negative raw dependency_composite, i.e. stronger composite dependency. For direction +1 TP53-loss and KEAP1-loss claims, a positive signed estimate means the loss group has a less negative raw dependency_composite, i.e. weaker composite dependency. For interaction claims with direction -1, a positive signed estimate means the claimed exposed-versus-comparator contrast is stronger in the named subgroup than in its complement. The effect-size threshold is 0.15 composite dependency-score units in the claimed direction.\n\nThe core findings are already robust: SMARCA4 loss is associated with substantially stronger dependency, while TP53 loss and KEAP1 loss are associated with substantially weaker dependency, with replication in broad samples, Avana/adherent settings, and KY-library samples. The main remaining uncertainty is whether the secondary PTEN association generalizes beyond Avana-associated technical contexts. H201, PTEN loss in non-Avana libraries, remains the priority registered untested comparison and should be followed up.\n\nI propose three new core-predictor tests in the combined non-Avana population: SMARCA4 loss direction -1, TP53 loss direction +1, and KEAP1 loss direction +1. These extend the KY-library replication to all non-Avana libraries, including the small Humagne_CD and other library groups. Expected signs are the same as the accepted overall effects. Accepted threshold-exceeding effects here would strengthen library-independent robustness within this dataset; unresolved attenuated effects would suggest heterogeneity outside Avana/KY but would not overturn the large accepted KY and Avana findings because the non-Avana population is smaller and more heterogeneous.\n\nFor PTEN, I add a broad interaction testing whether the PTEN-loss contrast differs between Avana-library and non-Avana-library samples. This is a more direct test of technical context than a main effect in non-Avana alone. Subgroup Avana, comparator/non-Avana complement in the full population; with direction -1, a positive signed interaction would mean PTEN loss is associated with stronger dependency in Avana than outside Avana. I expect a possible positive interaction because the best-supported PTEN effects were found in Avana/adherent core-intact settings, but null or reversed results are plausible alternatives if the PTEN association is not Avana-specific or if broad non-Avana samples are diluted by small library groups.\n\nI also propose a biologically motivated PTEN interaction with high EMT score, emt_score >= 0.5. PTEN loss activates PI3K-AKT-related signaling, and mesenchymal-state samples may be more sensitive to that pathway, so the PTEN-loss association could be stronger in the high-EMT subgroup. This is speculative relative to the core predictors. Alternatives are no modification or an opposite direction if epithelial or high-EMT states compensate through alternate dependencies. This adds a meaningful biological context test rather than repeating rejected technical modifiers such as Cas9 activity, lineage, aneuploidy, growth rate, or culture type. All conclusions remain dataset-specific associations on a composite dependency endpoint and do not establish causal knockout biology or clinical generalizability.",
"research_notes":"Core robust dataset-specific findings: SMARCA4 loss stronger dependency; TP53 loss weaker dependency; KEAP1 loss weaker dependency. These are large and replicated across overall, Avana/adherent, and KY contexts. PTEN loss has a narrower supported context, especially H178 core-intact Avana/adherent, with validation directionally positive but adjusted lower bound below threshold; broad PTEN remains unresolved. CDKN2A not robust, MTAP null, constructed markers/signature interactions unsupported. Current next priorities: H201 non-Avana PTEN main effect remains active and important. New registered follow-ups: core SMARCA4/TP53/KEAP1 effects in combined non-Avana libraries to test library-level robustness beyond KY; broad PTEN Avana-versus-non-Avana interaction to test technical context dependence; PTEN interaction with high EMT score as a biological context test. Interpretation remains associative and not causal/clinically generalizable."
